1
00:00:01,080 --> 00:00:03,548
We use moral persuasion,
and then we barbecue them.

2
00:00:05,640 --> 00:00:08,791
Are you going to personally prosecute
the case against Nicholas Baron?

3
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:10,439
That's exactly what I'm saying.

4
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:11,669
Hello?

5
00:00:12,360 --> 00:00:14,920
All we want is justice.

6
00:00:15,080 --> 00:00:17,913
Whenever I find who's responsible
for endangering my daughter,

7
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:20,753
I'm gonna feed him to the District
Attorney piece by piece.

8
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:23,229
- Could be disbarred for this.
- You were saying?

9
00:02:24,440 --> 00:02:26,795
<i>--by the homecoming</i>
<i>of Nicholas Baron,</i>

10
00:02:26,960 --> 00:02:30,350
<i>the reputed gangland leader known</i>
<i>sometimes as ''The Baron, ''</i>

11
00:02:30,520 --> 00:02:33,512
<i>who was paroled yesterday</i>
<i>after seven years in prison.</i>

12
00:02:34,640 --> 00:02:36,631
Turn that up.

13
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:38,756
<i>--extortion to conspiracy</i>
<i>to commit murder.</i>

14
00:02:38,920 --> 00:02:41,718
<i>Baron was convicted only</i>
<i>of tax evasion,</i>

15
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:43,871
<i>but his imprisonment was enough,</i>
<i>authorities say,</i>

16
00:02:44,040 --> 00:02:46,873
<i>to loosen the grip the Baron family</i>
<i>had on organised crime</i>

17
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:48,473
<i>operations in this area.</i>

18
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:51,871
<i>Advanced age and failing health</i>
<i>were reportedly factors in his release.</i>

19
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:54,349
- Don't wait up for me.
<i>- Baron is 65.</i>

20
00:02:54,520 --> 00:02:56,317
<i>On the local weather front--</i>

21
00:03:30,080 --> 00:03:33,914
- Why don't you just take my car?
- Forget it!

22
00:03:49,560 --> 00:03:50,595
Donald!

23
00:04:56,680 --> 00:04:57,749
Jesus.

24
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:28,797
Sarah? Sarah?

25
00:05:29,800 --> 00:05:33,190
Damn. No, not you.

26
00:05:33,360 --> 00:05:36,158
Something in the air is playing havoc
with my nose.

27
00:05:36,600 --> 00:05:38,113
Hay fever.

28
00:05:39,240 --> 00:05:41,037
You take what?

29
00:05:42,520 --> 00:05:45,080
I see. So the idea is
the pills make you so sleepy

30
00:05:45,240 --> 00:05:46,468
you forget you got hay fever.

31
00:05:46,640 --> 00:05:50,155
Well, if I find somewhere to lie down,
I'll take one of them.

32
00:05:50,320 --> 00:05:53,551
Tell Charlene I'll be there
in a few minutes. Okay.

33
00:05:53,720 --> 00:05:54,755
Sorry to keep you waiting.

34
00:05:54,920 --> 00:05:57,434
That's all right. Actually, Mr. Matlock,
I was waiting for you.

35
00:05:59,200 --> 00:06:01,794
- Here.
- Oh, thank you. Thank you.

36
00:06:03,040 --> 00:06:07,079
My name is Paul Baron.
My father's Nicholas Baron.

37
00:06:07,240 --> 00:06:11,074
Somebody planted a car bomb
in Donald Kovacs' car this morning.

38
00:06:11,240 --> 00:06:14,357
He's dead. The police think
my father killed him,

39
00:06:14,520 --> 00:06:17,956
but he didn't do it.
But he would like to talk with you.

40
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:22,078
I'm sure Nicholas Baron
has lots of lawyers

41
00:06:22,240 --> 00:06:24,993
with far more experience
in this sort of thing than I.

42
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:27,399
He wants you.

43
00:06:28,280 --> 00:06:32,990
- Well, thanks, but I decline.
- No, it's all right. I've got plenty.

44
00:06:33,160 --> 00:06:34,957
You talk with my father,

45
00:06:35,120 --> 00:06:38,556
and whether or not you decide
to take the case, this is what you get.

46
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:41,953
- That's $20,000.
- That's what it says.

47
00:06:45,960 --> 00:06:47,439
You wanna go for a ride?

48
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:53,114
Air conditioned?

49
00:06:54,080 --> 00:06:56,958
I'll have to run it full blast.

50
00:07:10,440 --> 00:07:12,271
My father's right over there.

51
00:07:19,400 --> 00:07:22,472
Know anything about roses,
Mr. Matlock?

52
00:07:22,640 --> 00:07:25,712
Oh, a fair amount. I know you're
supposed to fertilise them,

53
00:07:25,880 --> 00:07:27,598
water them, and cut them.

54
00:07:27,760 --> 00:07:29,876
That's more than most people know.

55
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:33,316
This is a cultivar that I personally
helped to develop.

56
00:07:33,480 --> 00:07:36,438
I call it Irene's Ruby.

57
00:07:36,600 --> 00:07:40,036
Roses are a hobby
that became a business.

58
00:07:41,960 --> 00:07:45,032
I know a fair amount about you too,
Mr. Matlock.

59
00:07:45,200 --> 00:07:48,431
Born in some backwater town
that doesn't even exist anymore,

60
00:07:48,600 --> 00:07:51,433
poor family,
father killed in a car crash,

61
00:07:51,600 --> 00:07:54,160
and here you are, one of
the best-known lawyers in the country.

62
00:07:54,320 --> 00:07:56,675
Now, how do you explain that?

63
00:07:57,600 --> 00:07:59,079
Hard work.

64
00:07:59,520 --> 00:08:01,397
I believe it was pre-ordained.

65
00:08:01,560 --> 00:08:05,519
I think you simply followed along
the path that had been laid out for you,

66
00:08:05,680 --> 00:08:08,956
just like I followed along the path
that had been laid out for me.

67
00:08:09,960 --> 00:08:11,188
Looking back along that path,

68
00:08:11,360 --> 00:08:15,911
I can see some things
I regret having done.

69
00:08:16,080 --> 00:08:20,551
Some of the crimes that I did
I've already paid for in prison.

70
00:08:21,080 --> 00:08:24,629
All of the others I'll pay for
soon enough,

71
00:08:25,240 --> 00:08:28,232
but I did not murder
Donald Kovacs,

72
00:08:28,400 --> 00:08:31,517
and I refuse to pay
for the crimes of others.

73
00:08:32,040 --> 00:08:34,235
It's a matter of honour.

74
00:08:36,640 --> 00:08:38,676
I should return this.

75
00:08:39,480 --> 00:08:41,869
I don't charge for consultations.

76
00:08:42,320 --> 00:08:45,118
As for the case, I decline.

77
00:08:46,360 --> 00:08:48,316
Mr. Matlock,

78
00:08:48,480 --> 00:08:50,835
I'm gonna do something
I've never done before.

79
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:52,956
I'm gonna beg.

80
00:08:53,640 --> 00:08:57,269
Please clear my name.

81
00:08:57,440 --> 00:08:58,793
I didn't do this,

82
00:08:58,960 --> 00:09:01,918
and I want to stand trial
so people will know that.

83
00:09:02,320 --> 00:09:04,197
I want to do it soon

84
00:09:04,360 --> 00:09:07,397
because in about six months' time,
I'm gonna be dead.

85
00:09:10,040 --> 00:09:12,349
Please, Mr. Matlock.

86
00:09:22,120 --> 00:09:23,872
On one condition.

87
00:09:25,120 --> 00:09:28,476
The case will be handled my way
on my terms.

88
00:09:29,320 --> 00:09:31,470
I make all the decisions,

89
00:09:31,640 --> 00:09:34,552
and I take it all the way,
no matter what.

90
00:09:34,720 --> 00:09:39,840
All the way, no matter what,
or I'm out right now.

91
00:09:43,280 --> 00:09:45,430
You have my word.

92
00:09:46,520 --> 00:09:49,034
The first thing you're gonna do
is turn yourself in.

93
00:10:03,720 --> 00:10:06,518
All right. Anybody wanna
do the honours?

94
00:10:08,080 --> 00:10:09,991
Miranda him, Charlie,
then book him.

95
00:10:14,720 --> 00:10:18,395
All right, Frank. What have you got
on my client?

96
00:10:18,560 --> 00:10:19,788
Motive.

97
00:10:20,200 --> 00:10:23,158
Donald Kovacs was the man who,
according to your client,

98
00:10:23,320 --> 00:10:25,550
bribed the accountant whose testimony
sent him to prison.

99
00:10:25,720 --> 00:10:26,948
He's also the man who took over

100
00:10:27,120 --> 00:10:29,953
many of the Baron family's
illegitimate enterprises

101
00:10:30,120 --> 00:10:32,111
while said client was in prison.

102
00:10:33,960 --> 00:10:34,949
What's the matter?

103
00:10:35,360 --> 00:10:38,352
- I got a hay fever going.
- It's all right.

104
00:10:38,520 --> 00:10:40,272
We got Eddie A.

105
00:10:40,440 --> 00:10:42,396
Eddie Alonzo, the man
who made the bomb,

106
00:10:42,560 --> 00:10:45,199
and indeed planted the bomb
that killed Kovacs.

107
00:10:45,360 --> 00:10:49,399
Now, Eddie A. was an old friend
of the Baron family.

108
00:10:49,560 --> 00:10:51,278
I say was because
he's no longer with us.

109
00:10:51,440 --> 00:10:53,510
He was found shot to death
in a field this morning.

110
00:10:53,680 --> 00:10:55,989
Two bullets to the head.
Now, the phrase that comes to mind

111
00:10:56,160 --> 00:10:59,789
is ''gangland-style killing.''
Hey, but what are friends for?

112
00:10:59,960 --> 00:11:01,916
Now, we got mud from that field

113
00:11:02,080 --> 00:11:04,878
found embedded in the tyres
of a certain car.

114
00:11:07,560 --> 00:11:09,790
Could we get on
to the substantial part?

115
00:11:09,960 --> 00:11:12,394
No problem, because we got
an eyewitness

116
00:11:12,560 --> 00:11:16,553
who saw the driver of that certain car
shoot and kill Eddie Alonzo.

117
00:11:16,720 --> 00:11:19,996
We've also got this,
found at the scene of the crime,

118
00:11:20,160 --> 00:11:23,391
and this belongs to--
Guess who.

119
00:11:23,560 --> 00:11:27,269
Frank, do you really think my client
would murder his own friend?

120
00:11:27,440 --> 00:11:28,429
Come on, Counsellor.

121
00:11:28,600 --> 00:11:30,431
I think your client would murder
his own mother

122
00:11:30,600 --> 00:11:31,999
if it meant saving his hide.

123
00:11:32,160 --> 00:11:34,230
The Barons aren't a family.
They're a pack of wolves.

124
00:11:34,400 --> 00:11:37,949
- Here I am defending the head wolf.
- Right. But why?

125
00:11:38,120 --> 00:11:39,712
I don't know, Frank.

126
00:11:42,520 --> 00:11:44,715
I guess I'm just living right.

127
00:11:53,280 --> 00:11:56,158
Phillips, I can't get
a conviction on this turkey

128
00:11:56,320 --> 00:12:00,950
unless the wife will testify
that she saw him pull the trigger.

129
00:12:02,520 --> 00:12:06,911
If those detectives of yours spent
less time staring out their windows

130
00:12:07,080 --> 00:12:09,230
and more time chasing criminals,

131
00:12:09,400 --> 00:12:12,198
we could put a few more
of them away, couldn't we?

132
00:12:13,200 --> 00:12:17,432
Don't tell me how tough it is.
I was a cop, remember?

133
00:12:18,320 --> 00:12:20,470
Will you stop trying
to break my heart

134
00:12:20,640 --> 00:12:22,756
and get me some hard evidence?

135
00:12:22,920 --> 00:12:26,230
I don't care how you do it,
just get it!

136
00:12:31,280 --> 00:12:34,795
Are you as pleased with yourself
as you look?

137
00:12:36,640 --> 00:12:39,677
- Here's the goods on Nicholas Baron.
- Sit down.

138
00:12:50,720 --> 00:12:53,712
How long has your father
been governor?

139
00:12:55,960 --> 00:12:58,952
You know, if you'd have asked
before you helped yourself,

140
00:12:59,120 --> 00:13:00,838
I might have warned you.

141
00:13:05,680 --> 00:13:10,071
Max? He's almost deaf. Max!

142
00:13:10,760 --> 00:13:13,672
Yeah. Yeah, yeah.

143
00:13:13,840 --> 00:13:16,274
No, no, don't move.
I'll toss them to you.

144
00:13:18,040 --> 00:13:19,359
There, two...

145
00:13:21,160 --> 00:13:24,038
...and two more. Okay?

146
00:13:24,200 --> 00:13:25,872
You're a good boy, Max.
I love you.

147
00:13:26,880 --> 00:13:29,314
His hearing's really getting bad.

148
00:13:32,200 --> 00:13:33,599
Tell me something.

149
00:13:33,760 --> 00:13:35,512
What do you think
of the Baron case?

150
00:13:35,680 --> 00:13:37,989
- I think it's a lock, boss.
- So do I.

151
00:13:39,120 --> 00:13:43,159
I'm afraid you're gonna have to
take a back seat this time, Palmer.

152
00:13:43,640 --> 00:13:45,232
But who's gonna prosecute?

153
00:13:56,840 --> 00:13:59,070
I said when I became
District Attorney

154
00:13:59,240 --> 00:14:02,152
that I would break the back
of organised crime in this city,

155
00:14:02,320 --> 00:14:03,753
and this is part of that promise.

156
00:14:03,920 --> 00:14:06,150
Are you saying that you're going to
personally prosecute

157
00:14:06,320 --> 00:14:09,153
- the case against Nicholas Baron?
- That's exactly what I'm saying.

158
00:14:09,320 --> 00:14:10,878
You and Ben Matlock almost
came to blows

159
00:14:11,040 --> 00:14:12,632
last time y'all
met in a courtroom.

160
00:14:14,320 --> 00:14:16,356
I never listen to rumours.

161
00:14:17,560 --> 00:14:20,518
Is it true that you and District Attorney
McShane are bitter enemies?

162
00:14:20,680 --> 00:14:23,319
Well, let's just say I didn't vote for him.
Excuse me.

163
00:14:28,240 --> 00:14:30,310
Defendant waives time.

164
00:14:30,480 --> 00:14:34,155
Further reading of the indictment
pleads not guilty.

165
00:14:34,320 --> 00:14:37,198
May we proceed directly
to the issue of bail?

166
00:14:37,360 --> 00:14:40,716
Mr. McShane,
what is the state's position?

167
00:14:41,480 --> 00:14:45,678
We recommend the defendant be held
without bail until his trial.

168
00:14:45,840 --> 00:14:49,310
To free a man with his reputation
as a mobster,

169
00:14:49,480 --> 00:14:52,153
one who faces
two counts of murder,

170
00:14:52,320 --> 00:14:53,389
would be a slap in the face

171
00:14:53,560 --> 00:14:56,711
of every law-abiding citizen
in this community.

172
00:14:56,880 --> 00:15:00,839
Moreover, Nicholas Baron is a man
of considerable wealth and means,

173
00:15:01,440 --> 00:15:06,594
one who could, and I submit would,
flee at the first opportunity.

174
00:15:06,760 --> 00:15:12,357
I move that he remain in custody
till the time of his trial.

175
00:15:14,880 --> 00:15:20,159
Your Honour, my client
has no intention of fleeing.

176
00:15:21,040 --> 00:15:23,838
On the contrary,
he wants to stand trial,

177
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:26,468
and he wants to do so
as soon as possible.

178
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:30,037
No matter what his past,
no matter what his reputation,

179
00:15:30,200 --> 00:15:33,078
he's entitled to the rights
of any citizen.

180
00:15:33,240 --> 00:15:35,515
Your Honour, the parole board
of the state penitentiary

181
00:15:35,680 --> 00:15:38,558
saw fit to grant my client
an early release.

182
00:15:39,080 --> 00:15:40,957
They didn't see him
as a threat to society,

183
00:15:41,120 --> 00:15:45,477
they saw him as an ageing ill man
deserving pity.

184
00:15:45,640 --> 00:15:49,428
Surely the court of the people
is no less compassionate.

185
00:15:49,600 --> 00:15:52,592
Bail is set at $500,000.

186
00:15:52,760 --> 00:15:56,389
Preliminary hearing
is set for September 1 2th.

187
00:15:56,560 --> 00:15:58,710
Is that acceptable to the defence?

188
00:15:58,880 --> 00:15:59,995
Yes, Your Honour.

189
00:16:00,720 --> 00:16:04,030
- Mr. McShane?
- Prosecution agrees.

190
00:16:04,200 --> 00:16:06,475
Court will recess
till 2:30 this afternoon.

191
00:16:09,680 --> 00:16:12,797
Never thought I'd see you
in a courtroom again.

192
00:16:12,960 --> 00:16:15,872
Never thought I'd have
an opportunity like this.

193
00:16:19,480 --> 00:16:22,199
<i>Is it true that you and District Attorney</i>
<i>McShane are bitter enemies?</i>

194
00:16:22,360 --> 00:16:27,354
<i>- Let's just say I didn't vote for him.</i>
<i>- Do you wanna elaborate on that?</i>

195
00:16:27,520 --> 00:16:30,478
- How do I look?
- Oh, you look great.

196
00:16:30,640 --> 00:16:33,279
Your client looks suspiciously
like a gangster.

197
00:16:34,320 --> 00:16:35,753
- Bless you.
- Thank you.

198
00:16:36,640 --> 00:16:39,916
Whatever it is you're allergic to,
I sure hope it isn't flowers.

199
00:16:40,080 --> 00:16:44,596
Seems Nicholas Baron's friends,
or business associates, you might say,

200
00:16:44,760 --> 00:16:47,877
are delighted
to have you on their team.

201
00:16:52,280 --> 00:16:54,510
You, I take it, are not.

202
00:16:54,760 --> 00:16:56,990
I was under the impression
we were criminal lawyers, Dad,

203
00:16:57,160 --> 00:16:59,355
not criminals' lawyers.

204
00:17:00,720 --> 00:17:03,154
I understand. I'll take this one alone.

205
00:17:05,240 --> 00:17:07,435
I just wanna know why
you took the case.

206
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,036
Nicholas Baron
has his own lawyers.

207
00:17:11,200 --> 00:17:13,873
He could've gone anywhere,
but he came to me.

208
00:17:14,520 --> 00:17:19,196
I have to believe it's because
I have a reputation for being honest.

209
00:17:19,360 --> 00:17:22,158
Besides, he begged me.

210
00:17:22,320 --> 00:17:24,550
He begged me
to take this thing to trial.

211
00:17:24,720 --> 00:17:26,278
It was very hard for him.

212
00:17:26,840 --> 00:17:29,559
Try as I might,
I can't get around that.

213
00:17:29,720 --> 00:17:31,392
I just can't get around it.

214
00:17:31,880 --> 00:17:36,351
He wants an honest defence,
I have to give him the best that I can.

215
00:17:37,440 --> 00:17:39,158
If you're in, so am I.

216
00:17:42,120 --> 00:17:43,917
I'll see you at 1 1 :00
at the Baron estate.

217
00:17:44,080 --> 00:17:45,354
All right.

218
00:17:47,040 --> 00:17:48,792
Hey, Ben.

219
00:17:48,960 --> 00:17:53,033
Ben, you'll never guess what I heard
about you down at the police station.

220
00:17:53,200 --> 00:17:56,397
Does it have anything to do with
my being Nicholas Baron's lawyer?

221
00:17:56,560 --> 00:17:59,074
- You're kidding.
- Guess again.

222
00:17:59,240 --> 00:18:01,231
He's not kidding.

223
00:18:01,400 --> 00:18:03,675
Tyler, we need you to check out
an Edward Alonzo.

224
00:18:03,840 --> 00:18:06,070
Apparently, he's the man
who put the bomb in Kovacs' car.

225
00:18:06,240 --> 00:18:07,753
He's known on the streets
as Eddie A.

226
00:18:08,600 --> 00:18:12,559
- We're talking major underworld here.
- Well, better get started.

227
00:18:22,960 --> 00:18:24,757
I'm Ben Matlock.
I have an appointment.

228
00:18:53,080 --> 00:18:56,152
Hello. I could've told you you'd wind up
taking the case. I'm Cathy Baron.

229
00:18:56,320 --> 00:18:57,912
Turn around and place
your hands on the--

230
00:18:58,080 --> 00:18:59,672
Leave him, Marty.

231
00:18:59,840 --> 00:19:02,195
This is Benjamin Matlock,
Father's attorney.

232
00:19:02,360 --> 00:19:04,316
Come in, please.

233
00:19:04,880 --> 00:19:06,313
I guess everybody's a little nervous.

234
00:19:06,480 --> 00:19:07,993
I'm searching everything
that moves.

235
00:19:08,160 --> 00:19:10,276
It's my brother Paul's idea.

236
00:19:10,880 --> 00:19:13,952
How many times do I have to tell you?
You didn't walk today, did you?

237
00:19:14,120 --> 00:19:17,078
I had other things to worry about
this morning.

238
00:19:17,240 --> 00:19:19,959
Mr. Matlock. Welcome.

239
00:19:20,120 --> 00:19:23,317
I'd like you to meet
my younger son, Daniel.

240
00:19:23,480 --> 00:19:26,040
- You're a doctor?
- Internist.

241
00:19:26,200 --> 00:19:28,919
You know, I've been having
an awful problem

242
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:31,116
with hay fever lately.
I don't suppose you--

243
00:19:31,280 --> 00:19:33,316
I'm sorry.
I only prescribe for patients.

244
00:19:35,520 --> 00:19:37,431
The cardiologist is gonna
stop by this afternoon.

245
00:19:37,600 --> 00:19:39,238
- You'll be here?
- No.

246
00:19:39,400 --> 00:19:41,277
I have other patients too,
you know.

247
00:19:41,440 --> 00:19:43,635
- Nice meeting you.
- Same here.

248
00:19:44,560 --> 00:19:47,632
I thought this little get-together
was gonna involve the whole family.

249
00:19:47,800 --> 00:19:51,395
Some of us are more interested
in family business than others.

250
00:19:51,560 --> 00:19:54,028
Then again, some of us
are just not welcome.

251
00:19:54,200 --> 00:19:56,873
- How are you, Mr. Matlock?
- Fine.

252
00:19:57,040 --> 00:19:58,792
Did you talk to the D.A.?

253
00:19:58,960 --> 00:20:01,269
Yeah, and he says
he has an eyewitness

254
00:20:01,440 --> 00:20:03,237
who saw you shoot Eddie Alonzo.

255
00:20:03,680 --> 00:20:09,710
- That's impossible. I was in the park.
- Well, we need to cover all bases.

256
00:20:09,880 --> 00:20:12,314
Right now, in order to plant the seed
of reasonable doubt,

257
00:20:12,480 --> 00:20:15,631
we need to give some thought as to
who else might have killed him.

258
00:20:16,360 --> 00:20:17,998
Hi. Sorry to be late.

259
00:20:18,160 --> 00:20:20,469
Having car trouble lately
and had to take a taxi.

260
00:20:21,840 --> 00:20:24,832
Nicholas, Paul,
this is my daughter and partner,

261
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:26,991
- Charlene Matlock.
- How do you do?

262
00:20:27,160 --> 00:20:30,277
- Nicholas and Paul Baron.
- It's a pleasure.

263
00:20:32,640 --> 00:20:34,073
Please.

264
00:20:37,880 --> 00:20:40,348
- May I?
- By all means.

265
00:20:40,520 --> 00:20:43,717
- Anyone else?
- I'll have some. Thank you.

266
00:20:43,880 --> 00:20:45,199
Let me go ahead with this.

267
00:20:45,360 --> 00:20:48,955
Because the killer had access
to Nicholas' car and gun,

268
00:20:49,120 --> 00:20:52,237
he or she was probably
no stranger to this household.

269
00:20:52,400 --> 00:20:53,594
What are you saying?

270
00:20:53,760 --> 00:20:55,318
Just that I'd like
to have a word or two

271
00:20:55,480 --> 00:20:57,072
with your help
and any business associates

272
00:20:57,240 --> 00:20:58,309
who might come by often.

273
00:20:58,480 --> 00:20:59,595
No.

274
00:20:59,760 --> 00:21:02,069
Mr. Matlock,
we take care of our own here.

275
00:21:02,240 --> 00:21:04,390
That's why our associates--

276
00:21:04,560 --> 00:21:06,710
Well, the family business
over the past several years

277
00:21:06,880 --> 00:21:08,677
has been reduced
to a small trucking company,

278
00:21:08,840 --> 00:21:10,796
and we're talking to our employees.

279
00:21:10,960 --> 00:21:12,916
That's not entirely accurate.

280
00:21:13,080 --> 00:21:15,355
- It's not?
- No.

281
00:21:15,520 --> 00:21:16,919
Besides owning
the trucking company,

282
00:21:17,080 --> 00:21:19,275
you also have partial ownership
in three restaurants,

283
00:21:19,440 --> 00:21:21,954
five country clubs,
two refuse-removal companies,

284
00:21:22,120 --> 00:21:24,475
and a host of flower shops.
Sugar?

285
00:21:24,640 --> 00:21:26,995
No, no sugar.
And that's true, by the way.

286
00:21:27,160 --> 00:21:29,469
We have a few minor
legitimate diversifications.

287
00:21:29,640 --> 00:21:32,837
There is one thing about this case
that bothers me.

288
00:21:33,720 --> 00:21:35,756
The man who killed Kovacs

289
00:21:35,920 --> 00:21:38,912
and then was himself killed,
Eddie Alonzo,

290
00:21:39,080 --> 00:21:41,640
was someone
whom all of you knew.

291
00:21:41,800 --> 00:21:43,518
Of course we knew him.

292
00:21:43,680 --> 00:21:46,797
He was one of my father's
oldest employees.

293
00:21:46,960 --> 00:21:49,269
He bounced all three of us
on his knee.

294
00:21:49,440 --> 00:21:51,954
Are you saying that one of us
hired him and then had him killed?

295
00:21:52,120 --> 00:21:55,556
He was like a brother to me,
like a second father to the children.

296
00:21:55,720 --> 00:21:59,156
- Besides, he was retired.
- I know.

297
00:21:59,320 --> 00:22:01,959
But who could best
talk him out of retiring?

298
00:22:02,120 --> 00:22:03,712
I don't think a stranger.

299
00:22:04,160 --> 00:22:06,435
I think someone
he knew and trusted.

300
00:22:06,600 --> 00:22:09,160
Well, if you'll excuse us,
we'll go to work.

301
00:22:09,320 --> 00:22:12,278
- Let's get a line on the eyewitness.
- Could I see you for a minute, please?

302
00:22:12,520 --> 00:22:14,556
I'll see you outside.

303
00:22:16,720 --> 00:22:19,996
Anything you have to say to
Mr. Matlock, you say in front in me.

304
00:22:21,760 --> 00:22:25,150
- Well?
- I want him off this case.

305
00:22:25,840 --> 00:22:27,990
I've been against hiring an outsider
from the beginning

306
00:22:28,160 --> 00:22:30,799
and nothing he has said or done
has made me change my mind on that.

307
00:22:30,960 --> 00:22:33,190
You've made your point, Paul,
but I'm going to ignore it.

308
00:22:33,360 --> 00:22:36,352
Mr. Matlock is my lawyer,
and that's the way it's going to be.

309
00:22:45,600 --> 00:22:51,072
- Cathy? You must be Cathy Baron.
- Must I?

310
00:22:51,240 --> 00:22:53,549
I've seen your picture in the
newspaper. Charlene Matlock.

311
00:22:53,720 --> 00:22:55,472
- I'm working on your father's case.
- Hello.

312
00:22:55,640 --> 00:22:58,996
For the moment, I'm without wheels.
Can I get a ride into town?

313
00:22:59,720 --> 00:23:02,154
- Sure.
- Thanks.

314
00:23:03,560 --> 00:23:05,391
Tell my dad I have a lift.

315
00:23:06,520 --> 00:23:10,035
- Don't forget your seat belt.
- Thanks.

316
00:23:29,200 --> 00:23:32,317
- Do you always drive this fast?
- Sorry.

317
00:23:32,480 --> 00:23:33,674
That's all right.

318
00:23:35,560 --> 00:23:39,269
So you're a successful lawyer.
I'm envious.

319
00:23:39,440 --> 00:23:42,989
Right now, I'm just a successful junior
partner, but I'm working on it.

320
00:23:43,160 --> 00:23:44,878
That's a lovely locket.
Is it old?

321
00:23:45,960 --> 00:23:47,871
It's my mother's.

322
00:23:50,200 --> 00:23:52,873
God, it feels good
to get out of that house.

323
00:23:53,040 --> 00:23:54,553
Still live at home?

324
00:23:55,160 --> 00:23:56,957
When Nicholas Baron is your father,

325
00:23:57,120 --> 00:23:59,998
you don't exactly have
a whole lot of options in life.

326
00:24:00,560 --> 00:24:02,790
- I'll give you a perfect example.
- What?

327
00:24:02,960 --> 00:24:06,270
- Do you see that cop up there?
- Yeah.

328
00:24:06,440 --> 00:24:08,590
Okay.

329
00:24:17,360 --> 00:24:18,429
See what I mean?

330
00:24:18,600 --> 00:24:22,593
I can't even get a cop interested in me.
It's very limiting.

331
00:24:22,760 --> 00:24:26,833
- That's tough.
- So how's the case going?

332
00:24:27,840 --> 00:24:31,879
- Well, it's a little early to speculate.
- Please do.

333
00:24:32,040 --> 00:24:33,871
All right.

334
00:24:34,040 --> 00:24:37,953
Whoever killed Eddie Alonzo had to
have access to your father's house.

335
00:24:38,520 --> 00:24:40,590
You mean like one of us.

336
00:24:41,240 --> 00:24:43,356
We can't rule that out.

337
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:48,353
You'd better learn one thing fast,

338
00:24:48,520 --> 00:24:50,636
and that's no matter what,
we are a family here,

339
00:24:50,800 --> 00:24:54,315
and no outsider dare question
our loyalty to each other ever.

340
00:24:54,480 --> 00:24:56,516
I can understand that, Cathy.

341
00:24:56,680 --> 00:24:58,750
Like you have to understand
that we're not on the case

342
00:24:58,920 --> 00:25:00,433
to protect your family.

343
00:25:00,600 --> 00:25:02,113
We're here
to defend your father.

344
00:25:04,720 --> 00:25:08,315
Now, do I still get a ride
or do I have to walk?

345
00:25:09,120 --> 00:25:11,156
Okay, you're right. We're even,

346
00:25:12,160 --> 00:25:13,798
but hang on.

347
00:25:58,640 --> 00:26:02,155
Tyler? Tyler, it's me, Ben.

348
00:26:09,440 --> 00:26:11,158
For crying out loud,
what are you expecting?

349
00:26:11,320 --> 00:26:12,958
Eddie Alonzo's ghost?

350
00:26:13,120 --> 00:26:15,270
Somebody killed him,
and that somebody might be looking

351
00:26:15,440 --> 00:26:18,750
for somebody who knew him,
and I am here in his apartment.

352
00:26:19,720 --> 00:26:21,233
- Did you find anything?
- Yeah.

353
00:26:21,400 --> 00:26:23,311
I found some escrow papers.

354
00:26:23,480 --> 00:26:25,948
Eddie Alonzo bought himself
a house ten days ago.

355
00:26:26,120 --> 00:26:27,712
A house?

356
00:26:27,880 --> 00:26:29,632
Sounds like he came
into some money recently.

357
00:26:29,800 --> 00:26:32,030
You think that money
came from the hit?

358
00:26:32,200 --> 00:26:33,553
I don't know.

359
00:26:35,240 --> 00:26:39,233
Nicholas Baron and Eddie Alonzo
go back almost 50 years.

360
00:26:39,400 --> 00:26:42,312
I don't think the Baron
would have to pay him.

361
00:26:42,480 --> 00:26:44,311
What a guy.
Now can we get out of here?

362
00:26:44,480 --> 00:26:45,993
I'll tell you what to do.

363
00:26:46,160 --> 00:26:49,311
See if you can find some current
pictures of the Baron's children.

364
00:26:49,480 --> 00:26:53,234
Maybe somebody in this building
saw them with Eddie Alonzo recently.

365
00:26:53,400 --> 00:26:54,549
Okay.

366
00:27:12,280 --> 00:27:13,759
You were saying?

367
00:27:20,960 --> 00:27:24,157
We're dealing with
professional criminals here.

368
00:27:24,320 --> 00:27:26,390
The Baron and his family,
the victim and his family--

369
00:27:26,560 --> 00:27:27,913
I mean, these guys are all pros.

370
00:27:28,080 --> 00:27:29,957
And I'll bet you that
that sniper was, too.

371
00:27:30,120 --> 00:27:32,315
If he was such a pro,
why am I still alive?

372
00:27:32,480 --> 00:27:34,550
Because nine times out of ten,

373
00:27:34,720 --> 00:27:36,711
Kovacs wanted
to scare you off the case.

374
00:27:36,880 --> 00:27:39,269
A case you had no business
being on in the first place.

375
00:27:39,440 --> 00:27:40,668
- Want some coffee?
- No.

376
00:27:40,840 --> 00:27:43,673
See, they're all afraid that you're
gonna see to it that this guy walks

377
00:27:43,840 --> 00:27:46,229
and that the old man just might try
and recoup a few

378
00:27:46,400 --> 00:27:50,029
of the business losses the family
suffered while he was in prison.

379
00:27:50,200 --> 00:27:53,351
Yeah. The trucking company
did go downhill, didn't it?

380
00:27:53,520 --> 00:27:55,351
The trucking company?

381
00:27:55,520 --> 00:27:57,909
Come on, these guys
are into numbers.

382
00:27:58,080 --> 00:28:01,197
They're into so many rackets,
they can't even keep track of them.

383
00:28:01,360 --> 00:28:04,670
And now the word is
that the Baron has some

384
00:28:04,840 --> 00:28:08,992
moral objections to dealing in dope.
Give me a break.

385
00:28:09,160 --> 00:28:12,709
I'll tell you what happened.
The Kovacs cornered the market first

386
00:28:12,880 --> 00:28:16,475
and then muscled them out, and if
Donald Kovacs had had any smarts,

387
00:28:16,640 --> 00:28:21,794
he would've stopped there. But no,
he got greedy and they got even.

388
00:28:21,960 --> 00:28:23,871
If that's the case,
the Kovacs had to expect

389
00:28:24,040 --> 00:28:26,554
some kind of reprisal
when the old man got out.

390
00:28:26,720 --> 00:28:30,429
They probably did, but they didn't
expect the reprisal so soon.

391
00:28:32,120 --> 00:28:33,712
Well, I guess the best thing
for me to do

392
00:28:33,880 --> 00:28:35,836
is go over there and have
a talk with them.

393
00:28:36,840 --> 00:28:37,909
The Kovacs?

394
00:28:40,120 --> 00:28:43,078
I'll call the coroner and have him
keep an eye out for you.

395
00:28:43,600 --> 00:28:45,192
Talk to the Kovacs.

396
00:29:14,200 --> 00:29:17,192
Now, that's the way azaleas
are supposed to look.

397
00:29:17,360 --> 00:29:18,873
What kind of fertiliser do you use?

398
00:29:19,760 --> 00:29:20,988
What'd you say?

399
00:29:22,040 --> 00:29:24,759
I said fertiliser.
What kind do you use?

400
00:29:25,520 --> 00:29:28,239
A little of this, a little of that.

401
00:29:30,240 --> 00:29:31,753
Trade secret, huh?

402
00:29:37,720 --> 00:29:43,590
- Mr. Matlock, come in. Phil Kovacs.
- Thank you. Nice house.

403
00:29:43,760 --> 00:29:45,273
Beautiful grounds.

404
00:29:45,440 --> 00:29:48,750
I was asking your gardener
how he does it.

405
00:29:48,920 --> 00:29:50,239
I didn't get very far.

406
00:29:50,400 --> 00:29:52,834
Well, he does have a little problem
with his hearing.

407
00:29:53,000 --> 00:29:54,035
A problem with his hearing

408
00:29:54,200 --> 00:29:57,636
or a problem with trying
to be something he isn't?

409
00:29:57,800 --> 00:30:00,712
No harm in having someone
keep an eye out front.

410
00:30:00,880 --> 00:30:04,316
- Yes. Is Mrs. Kovacs here?
- No.

411
00:30:04,720 --> 00:30:07,792
You did indicate that
you wanted to talk with me.

412
00:30:07,960 --> 00:30:08,995
Can I offer you a drink?

413
00:30:09,680 --> 00:30:10,908
What's your pleasure?

414
00:30:12,440 --> 00:30:14,351
Maybe a glass of mineral water.

415
00:30:15,400 --> 00:30:17,755
I understand you used to help
your brother run his business.

416
00:30:17,920 --> 00:30:20,957
- Sort of second in command.
- That's right.

417
00:30:21,720 --> 00:30:23,358
Any idea who
might have taken a shot

418
00:30:23,520 --> 00:30:25,511
at my associate and me
this afternoon?

419
00:30:26,720 --> 00:30:28,790
Took a shot at you?

420
00:30:30,280 --> 00:30:33,636
Absolutely not.
Why would we do that?

421
00:30:34,640 --> 00:30:36,312
If somebody frightened me
off this case,

422
00:30:36,480 --> 00:30:38,277
nobody else worth a damn
will take it.

423
00:30:39,080 --> 00:30:42,914
Nicholas Baron would be convicted.
I think you'd like that.

424
00:30:44,400 --> 00:30:47,312
You know, I heard prison
got to the old man.

425
00:30:47,480 --> 00:30:49,118
Made him soft.

426
00:30:49,280 --> 00:30:51,714
If I had to guess,
I'd say it was his son.

427
00:30:51,880 --> 00:30:53,472
Paul?

428
00:30:53,640 --> 00:30:56,712
He'd like nothing better
than to start a war with us.

429
00:30:56,880 --> 00:30:59,792
It's the only prayer he's got of
getting back in the major leagues.

430
00:31:00,720 --> 00:31:05,236
- Like you say, it's only a guess.
- That's why we want you on the case.

431
00:31:05,720 --> 00:31:09,315
To prove the Baron innocent,
you gotta find out who's guilty.

432
00:31:11,240 --> 00:31:12,992
What happens to the individual?

433
00:31:14,120 --> 00:31:17,157
All we want is justice.

434
00:31:22,240 --> 00:31:25,357
Hello, Mr. Matlock.

435
00:31:26,160 --> 00:31:29,516
You said you only prescribe
for patients. I'm a patient.

436
00:31:29,680 --> 00:31:31,989
Hay fever's worse than ever.

437
00:31:38,440 --> 00:31:41,432
- Something you get every year?
- No, first time.

438
00:31:42,040 --> 00:31:44,110
My nose is running,
my eyes is watery,

439
00:31:44,280 --> 00:31:46,475
the back of my throat itches.
I'm sneezing constantly.

440
00:31:46,640 --> 00:31:48,039
- It's driving me crazy.
- Say:

441
00:31:53,400 --> 00:31:55,470
You should be seeing an allergist,
not me.

442
00:31:55,640 --> 00:31:57,278
I thought I'd throw business
your way.

443
00:31:57,440 --> 00:32:00,034
From the looks of your waiting room,
you could use it.

444
00:32:01,000 --> 00:32:02,228
Well,

445
00:32:02,400 --> 00:32:06,439
having Nicholas Baron for a father
is not exactly good for business.

446
00:32:06,600 --> 00:32:10,275
Having him for a client
isn't good for business, either.

447
00:32:12,040 --> 00:32:13,758
It took me five years to build up
a practise

448
00:32:13,920 --> 00:32:15,831
and one day for my father
to destroy it.

449
00:32:16,000 --> 00:32:17,956
I'm gonna give you a shot.
I'll need a hip.

450
00:32:22,960 --> 00:32:27,431
- You think your father's guilty?
- I think he was framed.

451
00:32:27,600 --> 00:32:28,794
I do too.

452
00:32:29,840 --> 00:32:32,718
- By somebody in our family?
- Maybe.

453
00:32:34,320 --> 00:32:38,313
Just for the record, it's an allegation
I deeply resent.

454
00:32:40,680 --> 00:32:41,908
I can tell.

455
00:32:46,040 --> 00:32:48,873
- These just came.
- Take them to the children's hospital.

456
00:32:49,040 --> 00:32:50,871
These are for Charlene.

457
00:32:51,520 --> 00:32:53,829
- Thank you.
- Who are they from?

458
00:32:54,680 --> 00:32:56,716
Paul Baron.
I'm having dinner with him tonight.

459
00:32:58,640 --> 00:32:59,959
So I can interview him.

460
00:33:02,160 --> 00:33:03,832
Edward Alonzo.

461
00:33:04,000 --> 00:33:06,309
According to one of the tellers
at his bank, the only thing

462
00:33:06,480 --> 00:33:08,630
Edward Alonzo has deposited
in the last nine years

463
00:33:08,800 --> 00:33:10,438
is his social security cheques.

464
00:33:10,600 --> 00:33:12,670
But guess what happened
last week?

465
00:33:12,840 --> 00:33:14,432
He walked in with a cashier's cheque.

466
00:33:14,600 --> 00:33:17,831
$25,000, which he withdrew
the very next day

467
00:33:18,000 --> 00:33:20,514
and used it as a down payment
on a house for his sister.

468
00:33:22,680 --> 00:33:25,114
Early tomorrow morning,
I want you to go down to the park

469
00:33:25,280 --> 00:33:26,918
where Nicholas Baron walks.

470
00:33:27,080 --> 00:33:29,594
See if you can find somebody
who saw him there that morning.

471
00:33:29,760 --> 00:33:31,239
Okay.

472
00:33:32,960 --> 00:33:36,475
Well, the only way
we'll get this case dismissed

473
00:33:36,640 --> 00:33:38,312
is to discredit the D.A.'s eyewitness,

474
00:33:38,480 --> 00:33:41,199
and the only way to do that
is to get an eyewitness of our own.

475
00:33:46,440 --> 00:33:48,396
Relax, Ben. It's only an interview.

476
00:33:56,320 --> 00:33:58,276
That'll be all. Thank you.

477
00:33:59,000 --> 00:34:01,116
Hello. Look at you.
Don't you look nice.

478
00:34:01,280 --> 00:34:02,633
- Have a seat.
- Thank you.

479
00:34:04,240 --> 00:34:05,912
That's a very pretty dress.

480
00:34:06,080 --> 00:34:08,355
- You have nice taste.
- Thank you.

481
00:34:11,320 --> 00:34:14,949
They closed the doors tonight
at my request.

482
00:34:15,520 --> 00:34:17,078
I wanted to spare you any
embarrassment

483
00:34:17,240 --> 00:34:19,356
you might feel by being seen
with me socially.

484
00:34:21,520 --> 00:34:25,274
I also find the idea of
dining with you alone irresistible.

485
00:34:26,360 --> 00:34:27,759
How nice.

486
00:34:29,560 --> 00:34:34,395
So here's to a mutually
useful evening.

487
00:34:37,800 --> 00:34:39,119
How's your father?

488
00:34:39,280 --> 00:34:41,953
Convinced your father's innocent.
How's yours?

489
00:34:42,120 --> 00:34:43,872
Convinced your father
can get him off.

490
00:34:44,040 --> 00:34:45,758
Well, that was simple.
So much for business.

491
00:34:45,920 --> 00:34:49,037
You know what I think?
We should start off with the scampi,

492
00:34:49,520 --> 00:34:52,478
and then move right into the veal.
They've got a great veal dish here.

493
00:34:52,640 --> 00:34:53,629
It's their speciality.

494
00:34:53,800 --> 00:34:56,712
I hate to interrupt the festivities
with anything as mundane as the case,

495
00:34:56,880 --> 00:34:59,440
but we do need
a little bit more to go on.

496
00:34:59,600 --> 00:35:01,909
- We do?
- Yeah.

497
00:35:02,080 --> 00:35:04,594
- Like what?
- Well...

498
00:35:04,760 --> 00:35:07,638
- What about the Kovacs family?
- What about them?

499
00:35:07,800 --> 00:35:11,713
Could any one of them murdered
Donald Kovacs or Eddie Alonzo?

500
00:35:11,880 --> 00:35:15,475
- Any one of them.
- How do we prove that?

501
00:35:15,640 --> 00:35:17,835
Well, first, we have to get one
of them to confess.

502
00:35:18,920 --> 00:35:20,638
I don't think I wanna
know how you do that.

503
00:35:20,800 --> 00:35:22,358
Charlene, first, we talk.

504
00:35:22,520 --> 00:35:25,080
We're reasonable.
We use moral persuasion,

505
00:35:25,240 --> 00:35:26,639
and then we barbecue them.

506
00:35:28,360 --> 00:35:30,430
You're a dangerous man.

507
00:35:34,520 --> 00:35:36,875
That sounds
almost like a compliment.

508
00:35:37,640 --> 00:35:38,629
It is.

509
00:35:40,360 --> 00:35:41,713
Almost.

510
00:35:44,520 --> 00:35:47,353
- What?
- I have some questions

511
00:35:47,520 --> 00:35:49,112
about your family enterprises.

512
00:35:50,600 --> 00:35:52,352
Fill that out and send it
over to the office.

513
00:35:52,520 --> 00:35:56,354
That way I won't have to ask them
over dinner.

514
00:35:56,520 --> 00:36:00,877
I have to tell you I'm really not
in the mood for scampi or veal.

515
00:36:02,600 --> 00:36:06,593
Could I see a menu, please?

516
00:36:19,360 --> 00:36:20,509
Dad?

517
00:36:28,560 --> 00:36:30,118
Dad?

518
00:36:36,280 --> 00:36:38,236
- Hello?
<i>- Place is a mess, isn't it?</i>

519
00:36:38,400 --> 00:36:40,470
- Who--?
<i>- You should see your father.</i>

520
00:36:40,640 --> 00:36:43,393
<i>We're at the courthouse.</i>
<i>The side door's open.</i>

521
00:36:43,560 --> 00:36:45,790
<i>- And come alone.</i>
- Who is--?

522
00:37:01,800 --> 00:37:03,597
Dad?

523
00:37:04,160 --> 00:37:05,513
Dad?

524
00:37:12,840 --> 00:37:13,955
Hello?

525
00:37:25,120 --> 00:37:26,109
Hello?

526
00:37:26,280 --> 00:37:28,032
<i>Sorry to wake you up,</i>
<i>but there's an emergency</i>

527
00:37:28,200 --> 00:37:29,189
<i>down at the courthouse.</i>

528
00:37:29,360 --> 00:37:31,237
<i>That's where I'm holding</i>
<i>your daughter.</i>

529
00:37:37,600 --> 00:37:39,830
Charlene?

530
00:37:40,840 --> 00:37:42,239
Charlene?

531
00:38:06,040 --> 00:38:07,473
Hello?

532
00:38:08,200 --> 00:38:10,953
- Hello?
<i>- Try your office.</i>

533
00:38:16,280 --> 00:38:18,714
- Charlene?
- Dad?

534
00:38:28,760 --> 00:38:31,433
- Charlene?
- Dad?

535
00:38:31,600 --> 00:38:33,158
Are you all right?

536
00:38:33,320 --> 00:38:36,118
- I'm all right. Are you all right?
- I got this phone call.

537
00:38:39,480 --> 00:38:42,119
- Hello?
<i>- This is just a game.</i>

538
00:38:42,280 --> 00:38:45,033
<i>Walk off this case</i>
<i>while you still can walk.</i>

539
00:38:56,560 --> 00:38:59,597
Somebody really wants us
off this case.

540
00:39:06,720 --> 00:39:08,756
Somebody's trying to scare us
off this case.

541
00:39:08,920 --> 00:39:10,273
Let me tell you something.

542
00:39:10,440 --> 00:39:11,759
Now, I don't care who it is.

543
00:39:11,920 --> 00:39:15,356
Anything, I mean anything
happens to my daughter,

544
00:39:15,520 --> 00:39:17,317
there's gonna be hell to pay.

545
00:39:17,480 --> 00:39:19,471
- Our men will find out who did it.
- Paul, please.

546
00:39:19,640 --> 00:39:21,471
He's here because
he thinks it's one of us.

547
00:39:21,640 --> 00:39:22,914
That's impossible.

548
00:39:23,080 --> 00:39:25,833
Isn't Phillip Kovacs
the one you should be harassing?

549
00:39:26,000 --> 00:39:27,479
Let me tell you something.

550
00:39:27,640 --> 00:39:30,757
Whenever I find who's responsible
for endangering my daughter,

551
00:39:30,920 --> 00:39:34,595
I'm gonna feed him to the District
Attorney piece by piece.

552
00:39:41,080 --> 00:39:42,798
I don't know, Dad.
Call it intuition,

553
00:39:42,960 --> 00:39:44,951
call it pre-hearing jitters,
call it common sense,

554
00:39:45,120 --> 00:39:48,430
but I have this awful feeling
the D.A.'s gonna eat you alive.

555
00:39:48,600 --> 00:39:51,239
You mean it or you trying
to make me feel better?

556
00:39:51,400 --> 00:39:53,197
Dad, he has an eyewitness.
You have nothing.

557
00:39:53,360 --> 00:39:54,918
What are we gonna use
for a defence?

558
00:39:55,080 --> 00:39:57,992
- I wish I knew.
- Mr. Miracle.

559
00:39:58,160 --> 00:40:00,037
I want you to remember
this moment.

560
00:40:00,200 --> 00:40:03,476
Years from now, when you look back
on this time, when you're old

561
00:40:03,640 --> 00:40:06,438
and when you are a graceful matron
with 30 grandchildren,

562
00:40:06,600 --> 00:40:08,591
when criminal historians
study this case,

563
00:40:08,760 --> 00:40:10,398
I want you to remember this time,

564
00:40:10,560 --> 00:40:13,870
when things looked utterly dismal
and absolutely hopeless.

565
00:40:14,040 --> 00:40:17,715
I want you to remember the time
that Tyler Hudson single-handedly

566
00:40:17,880 --> 00:40:21,589
reached in and pulled your
fat out of the fire.

567
00:40:23,320 --> 00:40:25,151
Come in, Alice.

568
00:40:26,320 --> 00:40:30,598
Ben Matlock, Charlene Matlock,
Alice Jenkins.

569
00:40:30,760 --> 00:40:34,150
Alice was in Lincoln Park
panting a landscape

570
00:40:34,320 --> 00:40:37,517
on the very day and at the very time
the murders were committed.

571
00:40:37,680 --> 00:40:41,878
Guess who else was there.
Nicholas Baron. She saw him,

572
00:40:42,040 --> 00:40:45,077
and she's willing to say so in court.

573
00:40:45,640 --> 00:40:48,359
Alice, I am very happy to meet you.

574
00:40:54,760 --> 00:40:59,390
Now, Miss Jenkins,
do I understand correctly

575
00:40:59,560 --> 00:41:02,791
that you've been working
on this painting every day

576
00:41:02,960 --> 00:41:05,713
at Jackson Park for almost
two weeks now?

577
00:41:05,880 --> 00:41:08,474
Yes. You see, that is a study

578
00:41:08,640 --> 00:41:12,872
in the way the sun displays
and enhances these leaves

579
00:41:13,400 --> 00:41:14,833
and the mist on the water.

580
00:41:15,000 --> 00:41:18,470
I intend to call this <i>Sun Dance.</i>

581
00:41:19,120 --> 00:41:20,155
It's nice.

582
00:41:22,160 --> 00:41:26,278
Have you ever seen
Nicholas Baron before?

583
00:41:26,840 --> 00:41:30,469
Yes. As a matter of fact,
I saw him in Jackson Park

584
00:41:30,640 --> 00:41:34,030
because he walked in front of me
when I was painting

585
00:41:34,200 --> 00:41:36,760
on a week ago Wednesday.

586
00:41:37,320 --> 00:41:39,311
He was so handsome
and distinguished,

587
00:41:39,480 --> 00:41:41,835
I had to stop painting.

588
00:41:42,920 --> 00:41:45,388
How far away from you was he?

589
00:41:47,160 --> 00:41:48,752
Nine feet.

590
00:41:48,920 --> 00:41:52,356
- About what time of day was this?
- Close to 8:00.

591
00:41:52,520 --> 00:41:54,750
I was almost done for the day.

592
00:41:54,920 --> 00:41:58,879
That's about the time Mr. Henderson
said that Mr. Alonzo was shot.

593
00:42:00,000 --> 00:42:04,869
- Is this what you wanted?
- I'll let you know.

594
00:42:05,040 --> 00:42:06,792
You sure that's the man you saw?

595
00:42:06,960 --> 00:42:10,077
Yes, yes, because I saw his picture

596
00:42:10,240 --> 00:42:12,879
in one of those
newsweekly magazines.

597
00:42:13,040 --> 00:42:15,315
- And I said to myself:
- Yeah.

598
00:42:15,480 --> 00:42:20,395
''That's that handsome man,
and he just got out of prison.''

599
00:42:21,320 --> 00:42:24,596
So you're sure
that it was Nicholas Baron

600
00:42:24,760 --> 00:42:28,514
that walked in front of you while
you were painting in Jackson Park

601
00:42:28,680 --> 00:42:30,875
on the morning of the 1 9th
around 8:00?

602
00:42:31,600 --> 00:42:35,388
Oh, yes. I'm sure.
I will never forget it.

603
00:42:36,480 --> 00:42:38,277
No further questions.

604
00:42:44,240 --> 00:42:48,995
Miss Jenkins, do you know a man
by the name of Michael Escott?

605
00:42:49,440 --> 00:42:50,668
No.

606
00:42:52,120 --> 00:42:54,156
What about Anthony Berinni?

607
00:42:55,040 --> 00:42:57,838
- No.
- Irrelevant, Your Honour.

608
00:42:58,000 --> 00:43:01,788
Mr. McShane is confusing the witness,
and frankly, confusing me.

609
00:43:01,960 --> 00:43:05,589
I intend to show relevance
in a moment, Your Honour.

610
00:43:06,080 --> 00:43:07,399
Proceed.

611
00:43:08,560 --> 00:43:13,509
Miss Jenkins, do you
know a Robert Daily?

612
00:43:13,680 --> 00:43:17,719
- No.
- You don't?

613
00:43:18,720 --> 00:43:19,709
Well, that's odd.

614
00:43:19,880 --> 00:43:24,590
I say that because according to this
Atlanta police record,

615
00:43:24,760 --> 00:43:29,993
you do know them,
or at least you did.

616
00:43:31,400 --> 00:43:34,233
I don't know what
you're talking about.

617
00:43:34,400 --> 00:43:39,110
Isn't it true that these three men were
each charged with serious felonies,

618
00:43:39,280 --> 00:43:44,149
but in each case you came forward
and provided alibis.

619
00:43:46,280 --> 00:43:48,748
Isn't it true, Miss Jenkins,
that in fact you were paid

620
00:43:48,920 --> 00:43:52,230
in each of these instances
to provide an alibi?

621
00:43:54,040 --> 00:43:58,272
- I just like to help people.
- Just answer the question.

622
00:44:01,000 --> 00:44:05,915
Did you see Nicholas Baron in the park
the morning of the 1 9th?

623
00:44:07,320 --> 00:44:08,309
Well--

624
00:44:08,480 --> 00:44:11,074
Just a simple declarative sentence,
Miss Jenkins.

625
00:44:11,240 --> 00:44:14,550
- Tell the truth for a change.
- Your Honour?

626
00:44:14,720 --> 00:44:16,199
Did someone approach you

627
00:44:16,360 --> 00:44:19,875
to be a witness for the defence
in the case?

628
00:44:20,600 --> 00:44:21,794
Yes.

629
00:44:21,960 --> 00:44:25,748
Were you told to testify that you saw
Nicholas Baron in that park?

630
00:44:26,720 --> 00:44:30,474
- Yes.
- You lied, didn't you?

631
00:44:30,640 --> 00:44:32,835
I mean, you never saw him there,
did you?

632
00:44:33,320 --> 00:44:34,878
No.

633
00:44:35,040 --> 00:44:37,474
Did someone pay you to lie,
Miss Jenkins?

634
00:44:38,800 --> 00:44:40,119
Yes.

635
00:44:40,640 --> 00:44:43,108
Would you point him out, please?

636
00:44:45,320 --> 00:44:48,869
- The lawyer, Mr. Matlock.
- I beg your pardon?

637
00:44:50,000 --> 00:44:53,231
The lawyer, Mr. Matlock.

638
00:44:55,560 --> 00:44:57,471
Mr. Matlock?

639
00:45:00,880 --> 00:45:02,518
Mr. Matlock?

640
00:45:09,720 --> 00:45:13,076
I have no further use
for this witness.

641
00:45:29,320 --> 00:45:35,668
- Matlock could be disbarred for this.
- That's right, kid. That's right.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
